  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 AM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nMidlevel heights begin to rise across the central U.S, supporting \nsubsidence aloft and overall dry and quiet weather to build this \nweekend. Surface analysis depicts 1032mb high pressure centered over \nNorthern Ontario while 996mb low pressure over Manitoba pushes a \nweak warm front across Northern Michigan. Warm air advection \ncombined with a moderate pressure gradient across the Great Lakes \nRegion will support near record-breaking temperatures today along \nwith highs in the 70s through Monday with breezy southern flow. \n\nThe second half of the forecast period takes a sharp turn back to \nnormal October temperatures as the aforementioned rigging pattern \nbreaks down and quickly lowers heights across the Northern U.S. H8 \ntemperatures, currently around 20 degrees, quickly fall back into \nthe single digits, supporting highs in the 50s for most locations. \nAt the same time, a small wave develops lee of the Canadian Rockies \nand progresses to the Great Lakes Region Monday night. Light showers \nwill be observed across the CWA, but only a general tenth or less of \nQPF is expected. Another wave follows shortly behind and continues \nrain chances around the Thursday/Friday timeframe.\n   \nForecast Details:  \n\nToday, Sunday, and Monday: Conditions remain dry through the \nentirety of the weekend as any rain potential from the northern \nextent of Isaia's disheveled moisture plume remains confined to \nSouthern Michigan and the Ohio Valley.  Aforementioned warm air \nadvection will commence this morning as pressure gradient force \nwinds create breezy southern flow gusting well into the 20s. \nTemperatures appear to remain slightly lower compared to yesterday's \nguidance as a more southerly oriented fetch leads to less downslope \nheating. Regardless, near record breaking heat builds this afternoon \nwith highs in the mid/upper 70s. The apex of aforementioned ridging \nbreaks down this Sunday and slightly lowers temperatures around 70 \nthis Sunday and Monday.\n\nTuesday through Friday Outlook: A midlevel weak wave originating \nfrom Northern Alberta develops surface low pressure with an apparent \nstationary boundary and digs across the Great Lakes region Monday \nnight through Tuesday morning. Moisture tied to this system becomes \ncut off from its Pacific source and appears to be decaying as it \ntransits across the Great Lakes Region, resulting in pretty marginal \nQPF amounts. Highest total rainfall amounts remain around a tenth \ninch for most parts of the CWA. Latest ensembles appear to have much \nless rainfall for beyond the midweek as longwave troughing with \nclosed low pressure currently over the Pacific Northwest settles \nover the southwest U.S. next week and breaks down before reaching \nthe northwoods. Some moisture and subsequent rainfall can still be \nexpected at this time, but with much lower amounts compared to \nyesterday's guidance.",
